f  um macquarie is the concept artist and  then roger christian the set decorator  they they were all  you know they all got the concept it  created the concept it might have been  inspired by buck rogers like serialized  sci-fi tv but it was going to be more  grounded  with the drive to create a grittier  sci-fi universe george lucas brought on  board roger christian a young designer  who at the time was working on the 1930s  prohibition movie lucky lady christian  said that lucas didn't want anything in  star wars to stand out he wanted it all  to feel real and used a legitimate  lived-in universe designs and aesthetic  choices like this would bring a huge  depth of character to the world but the  challenge however was trying to realize  this vision with a budget of almost  nothing  christians said to work on the film  before it was even green lit by 20th  century fox paid out of lucas's own  pocket so as a result he had to get  creative iconic sets like the millennium  falcon interior and the death star's  trash compactor were cobbled together  from old airplane scrap metal bought in  bulk and assembled into backdrops that  would stand the test of time and this  idea of reusing and recycling old scrap  wasn't restricted to larger sets and  builds but was used to create smaller  props as well this led christian to the  crafty idea of reusing and repurposing  real working weapons in order to create  the arsenal of star wars  one of the first guns that christian  would show george lucas was in a shape  reminiscent of buck rogers sci-fi  classic xz-38 disintegrator pistol it  was a combination of real world german  mauser c96 broom handle and a handful of  spare parts combining to form one of the  most iconic firearms in the franchise  han solo's dl44 blaster  so han solo's famous blaster the dl-44  actually based on the mauser c96 that's  right so can you walk us through the  greebly's professional word that they  added i know you said they added a scope  but how did they sort of star  this for  for han solo the the production designer  visited visited the armorer and looked  for donor like host guns that they could  then attach stuff to and the easiest way  to change the outline of something  fairly iconic like a broom handle mauser  was to put a scope mount and a scope on  it now sacrilege for collectors this is  quite a nice  wartime commercial example they actually  had to drill a hole into the body of  this gun to then attach the mount for  the scope to then sit over the top of it  this was never meant to have a scope on  it but the bottom line was some sort of  scope some sort of muzzle attachment  which instead of being like a suppressor  or something was now a german mg81  muzzle  booster flash hider stuck on the front  they had to come up with a new chunky  welded on  barrel as well to support that and make  it not look too rubbish and moving  swiftly on from i suppose one side of  the alliance to the other is  boba fett's weapon of choice it doesn't  look like there's been a huge amount  changed in this weapon to  give boba his blaster there are a few  minor  tweaks cosmetic tweaks to it but i guess  because most of the audience wouldn't  know what this is which is a webby and  scott number one mark one flair launcher  break open shove in massive flare  close it up shoot it into the sky so  that kind of did as a generic laser  blaster just with a scope on it again to  give you that sci-fi look  but then for that was in obviously  empire strikes back but for return of  the jedi they gave it a whole new barrel  assembly with like ribbing and knurling  and more of a busy look to it with the  same basic gun as the base i think the  very iconic thing  is the sterling  which um went on to birth the e11  blaster  so i'll give you that version  and i  get to grab  the cool star wars wig  we can see a side by side of everything  that was done to  turn this into the empire's sort of main  infantry weapon of choice yeah which  isn't that much actually yeah we haven't  got the magazine fitted that obviously  that comes out here so is this one of  the  movie props that is a genuine st now all  of these were taken apart after filming  because they were a working armory they  hired stuff out so they took all the  grievances all of the stuff off but we  know because quite frankly the entire  inventory of sterling that baptism was  hired out for us for a new hope that  this is one of those guns and then it  was put back together sometime in the  90s with one of the original scopes one  of the original mags at least the same  stock of these plastic these were drawer  runners would you believe from a from a  hardware store i believe it's being cue  just stuffed in there to cover up the  cooling holes and make it look high-tech  and then for me the icing on the cake of  us having this in the national  collection of arms and armor is believe  it or not this slightly ropey looking  plastic box on the side because that's  not original to the gun but it was made  or acquired and added by lucasfilm when  they borrowed this and the rebel version  back from bapti  to redesign it for for force awakens oh  wow yeah so this was used as reference  so the full stop in its little movie  history is it was a reference piece to  create the f11d  the sort of rebooted version of the e11  so there we are beautiful piece of  cinema and firearms history that yep  good on sterling  of course the arsenal of star wars has  grown and developed in a number of  different directions since the very  first film back in 1977 and with the  ability to give fans the closest chance  to handle those classic firearms video  games have been at the forefront of that  development  star wars games have followed my love  for sci-fi and gaming across the years  as i'm sure they have for many others  from the 1995 first-person shooter star  wars dark forces to games like jedi  outcast and republic commando these  titles have formed some of my earliest  memories of being behind a virtual  blaster and across the decades  franchises and studios have brought  their own take to both existing star  wars weapons as well as creating their  own while star wars jedi 2 stuck pretty  close to the source material it did  allow players to get their hands on some  more iconic offerings like the wookie  bowcaster  and its rendition of the imperial e11  still had the recognizable form of the  sterling even with these early 2000s  graphics  republic commando helped popularize the  idea of interchangeable weapon systems  where the clone's default dc-17m weapon  system was able to reconfigure into  standard blaster assault rifles  compact sniper rifles  or even an anti-armor grenade launcher  this idea actually played nicely with  both other games and movies within the  franchise as we would go on to see other  configurable weapons like the rebel a280  series of blaster rifle  in terms of the balance between staying  true to the source material and creating  fun video game versions of these iconic  sci-fi weapons the games that do it best  in my opinion are dice's rebooted  battlefront titles the battlefront games  do a great job in making sure the vast  majority of their arsenal is fun to play  with as well as true in spirit to the  trilogies in which they come from and  for a game that plays out across  multiple generations of star wars lore  each with their own unique style and  flavor that is no mean feat  classic weapons like the t21 and the dlt  19 wonderfully match up to their  in-movie counterparts as well as keeping  the aesthetic of their inspiration in  this case the lewis gun and mg34 these  are weapons that we see used in the  movie not uncommonly but game designers  tweak and toy with what they are capable  of to make them more suited to different  roles classes and applications but in a  way that their operation is still close  enough to the source material yet far  enough removed that each one feels like  its own beast  there are also plenty of weapons in the  star wars universe that don't get their  share of the limelight in tv shows or  the movies but get a second life in  video games weapons like the scatter gun  first seen in an episode of the clone  wars or the jawa c87 ion blaster first  seen in the very briefest of shots  disabling r2d2 in a new hope while both  these weapons appear in blink and you'll  miss the moments on screen they are both  common sidearms in battlefront 2 and  they both still retain that real world  weaponry dna that christian and lucas  instilled in the franchise back in the  mid 70s the scatter gun taking cues from  the spaz 12 and the ion blaster from a  cut down lie enfield rifle  video games are always going to be an  integral part to the star wars fandom  but the challenge for developers working  on star wars games is balancing the  players expectation of these iconic  weapons while also creating something  new and exciting for players to play  around with so the big challenge for any  large franchise like star wars you have  to extrapolate from what everyone knows  what's established by say the first  movie and populate that universe with  more stuff the real world is is varied  and if in a sort of space cowboy setting  like star wars you're going to come  across lots of different weapons so  you're going to have to invent wholly  new types of weapon that fit the world  so it's a real challenge for i think for  developers to  meet expectations but also take things  forward i think star wars games have  mostly been successful with that the  first thing that comes to mind is the  briar pistol from dark forces the the  original star wars first person shooter  that never been seen before in a star  wars movie and even though that wasn't  based directly on a known world war two  guns say as as most star wars guns do  somehow it's still fitted so they made  they took a slight gamble there and  introduce something new and now you'll  see that blaster in other star wars  media as well so it's established itself  despite changes and challenges the  legacy of the original trilogy's arsenal  of firearms lives on they broke the  mould for sci-fi designs away from the  conventional ray guns of the 30s 40s and  50s and paved the way for more grounded  sci-fi weapons like the pulse rifle and  deckard's sidearm from blade runner star  wars showed that movies can create rich  and original science fiction universes  by pulling just a little bit from our  own reality  and the prequels sequels and spin-offs  to one degree or another still take  notes from those cobbled together guns  and spare parts that were brought to  life in the originals from the m60  machine gun in rogue one  the g36 assault rifle in the last jedi  or even din jaren's 19th century bergman  1894 pistol in the mandalorian  i believe that the arsenal of star wars  will always be at its best when it's  rooted in our reality  and for the team that tried to build  their sci-fi vision with an extra no  budget a box of prop weapons a pile of  scrap and some old airplane parts that's  a pretty impressive legacy  [Music]  thank you so much for watching this  week's episode of loadout if you enjoyed  please like the video and subscribe for  more and of course you can reach out to  me at a regular dave or let me know down  in the comments below what else you'd  like to see an episode on if you want to  see more video game firearms content  then you can watch our previous series  of this show or check out our weekly  series of expert reacts where i show our  friends the royal armories a slew of  guns to break down until next time i've  been your host dave dewitt thank you  again for watching and i'll see you in  the next episode  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

